{
  "dom_id": "step-q74672",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182550/assets/44e48aadd601b6b9c66358bac848748f6a752d2144df500dfb0dfcf6aec105da.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $17,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $Q$ on the plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $OQ<17$\n\nII. $OQ>17$\n\nIII. $OC>17$",
  "worked_solution": "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC=17\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $Q$ lies outside the circle. So, the distance between $Q$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOQ>OC=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true, while statements I and III are false.",
